Fix the node type of the stand-in built when tre_expand_ast() wraps an
expanded repetition that carries approximate matching parameters.

tre_expand_ast() rewrites each iteration node with a finite repeat count
into a catenated sequence of copies of itself, replacing the node in
place:

    node->obj = seq1->obj;
    node->type = seq1->type;      /* now CATENATION */

If that node also carries approximate matching parameters, as in
"(ab){2~1}" or "a{2+14}{2+4}", the code a few lines below wraps it in a
pair of PARAMETER literals, using a stand-in node for the content that
`node' had before the wrapping.  It builds that stand-in with a
hardcoded type:

    node_copy = tre_ast_new_node(mem, ITERATION, sizeof(tre_iteration_t));
    node_copy->obj = node->obj;

so node_copy is tagged ITERATION while holding a 16-byte
tre_catenation_t, which every later pass reads back as a 32-byte
tre_iteration_t.  Pass node->type instead.

With the TRE update in r90973 (PR#19331) this no longer reads out of
bounds where the fuzzer saw it, but tre_ast_to_tnfa() now asserts on the
garbage max, so every such pattern fails with "assertion 'iter->max ==
-1 || iter->max == 1' failed in executing regexp" (R maps TRE's assert()
to an R error).  With the fix, "(ab){2~1}c" and "a{2~1}{2~1}" compile
and match.  The other half of the earlier version of this patch, copying
the whole literal union in tre_copy_ast(), is fixed by the update.

Upstream status: kevinushey/tre branch bugfix/expand-ast-node-type, in
the r-integration branch at 4723ea5; r-devel/r-svn PR #537, which
re-vendors that commit (no Bugzilla report yet).
Delete this patch once the fix lands in R trunk (ossfuzz.sh will report
it as already-applied in the meantime, which is harmless).

diff --git a/src/extra/tre/tre-compile.c b/src/extra/tre/tre-compile.c
index 39eff64a0f..2b7d2be866 100644
--- a/src/extra/tre/tre-compile.c
+++ b/src/extra/tre/tre-compile.c
@@ -1006,7 +1006,7 @@ tre_expand_ast(tre_mem_t mem, tre_stack_t *stack, tre_ast_node_t *ast,
 		old_params[TRE_PARAM_DEPTH] = params_depth;
 		/* XXX - this is the only place where ast_new_node is
 		   needed -- should be moved inside AST module. */
-		node_copy = tre_ast_new_node(mem, ITERATION,
+		node_copy = tre_ast_new_node(mem, node->type,
 					     sizeof(tre_iteration_t));
 		if (!node_copy)
 		  return REG_ESPACE;
